<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <style name="Theme.SubTrack" parent="android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar" />

    <!--
      The theme the launcher activity starts in, and only that.

      It exists because of what phase 16g measured: the system splash showed the icon on #FAFAFA,
      then handed over to a window whose background was also #FAFAFA but carried nothing - a blank
      white frame the theme gate held on screen for 173-212 ms on API 34 - and only then did the
      app draw its own #D3E2D8. Three colours for one launch, the middle one empty. This theme
      removes the middle one by making the splash and the held window the same surface.

      The parent is explicit and overrides what the dotted name would otherwise imply: `Theme.X.Y`
      normally inherits from `Theme.X`, but this one inherits from the library instead. The name
      still reads as "the starting form of Theme.SubTrack", which is what it is - the activity
      leaves it through `postSplashScreenTheme` below.

      `windowSplashScreenBackground` is the icon's own ground, read from colors.xml rather than
      from Color.kt. That is the same one-directional rule ARCHITECTURE section 27 sets for the
      icon: the mark's colours are pinned where the launcher and the Play Store can reach them,
      and a later adjustment to a *theme* colour must not silently repaint them. The splash draws
      the icon, so it takes the icon's colour, not the scheme's.

      Dark, in both schemes, and that is the decision rather than an oversight. The foreground is
      twelve gold coins; gold measures 2.42:1 on white (Color.kt) and cannot carry a shape there,
      so a light splash would hide the mark. The cost is accepted: in the dark theme the splash
      hands over to the app with no colour change at all, and in the light theme there is one
      dark-to-light step at the end. One step, not two, and never an empty frame.

      No `windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor`: the foreground is already drawn on its own
      ground by this theme's background, and adding a second disc behind it would shrink the
      icon into the smaller of the two size classes Android 12 defines.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.SubTrack.Starting" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/ic_launcher_ground</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.SubTrack</item>
    </style>

</resources>
